From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:29:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Store index records with the records they index

https://w3c.github.io/IndexedDB/#store-a-record-into-an-object-store step 5 gives each index of the
store a record per key of the value: the key extracted with the index's key path, or each subkey for
a multiEntry index, and none if extracting fails, throws or gives an invalid key. Deleting records
and clearing the store delete their index records, and a key that a unique index has for another
record fails the put with a ConstraintError and no effects. Until now no index had records, unique
indexes accepted anything, and a multiEntry key path panicked in extract_key.

Script computes the keys, as Gecko, Chromium and WebKit do, so the storage thread never runs JS:
for put()/add() and cursor.update(), IDBObjectStore::index_keys extracts them from the clone for
every index of the connection's metadata (key.rs index_keys; convert_value_to_multientry_key skips
subkeys that are invalid or throw and removes duplicates). PutItem carries them as
`index_keys: Vec<IndexedDBIndexKeys { index_id, keys }>`, one entry per index. When the backend
generates the key, step 1.1.3 injects it into the value before step 5, which script can't do: the
parts of an index key that come from it are `IndexedDBIndexKey::PrimaryKey` placeholders, for a key
path equal to the store's and for such items of a sequence key path; an item below the store's key
path fails (the key is a number), and any other item gives what it gives on the value without the
key. The backend fills the placeholders with the record's key (Chromium and WebKit do the same).

The backend (queries.rs) keeps a unique index's records in unique_index_data and any other's in
index_data, Gecko's tables, read in (key, primary key) order. `ObjectStore` now lists the store's
indexes with their unique flag (one LEFT JOIN in object_store()). put_record checks that the keys are
for exactly the store's indexes (else the transaction fails: script's metadata isn't the database's),
encodes them without duplicates, and checks every unique key against other records before writing
anything, so a conflict (`PutItemResult::UniqueIndexKeyExists(index)`, a ConstraintError in script)
changes neither the record it would replace, nor other indexes, nor the key generator, and the
transaction goes on; the unique table's primary key is the backstop that fails the transaction.
Then it deletes the replaced record's index records, writes the record and inserts the new ones.
delete_records and clear delete the index records of the deleted records first. New indexes on
(object_store_id, object_data_key) of both tables find a record's index records and serve the host
SQLite's foreign keys; SCHEMA_VERSION 3, which resets existing databases (owner rule).

Script computes the keys with the transaction inactive, as it is while the value is cloned: the
spec extracts them from a clone in a user-agent defined realm, where no page code runs, but Servo
extracts them in the page's realm, where a getter that a hole of a multiEntry array or a missing
property reaches on a prototype does run. Requests from such a getter throw a
TransactionInactiveError instead of going before the one being made.

createIndex doesn't populate an index from existing records yet (P3.2), and indexes aren't read yet
(P3.3, P3.4). A sequence key path still evaluates to an ordinary object, so it gives no keys; the
next commit fixes that.

Validation: `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` (host SQLite): 122 passed, 1 skipped (P2 tip 115/1); new backend tests on every executor: index records after puts and overwrites, deletes of keys and ranges and clear, deleteIndex/deleteObjectStore and a reused index id, unique conflicts without any effect (record, other indexes, key generator, multiEntry, a record replaced with its own keys), placeholders filled with generated keys, abort, mismatched index keys. Host WPT tests/wpt/tests/IndexedDB with SpiderMonkey and this commit's binary: 3,298 of 4,511 (P2 tip 3,289), new passes idbobjectstore_add and idbobjectstore_put "unique:true" and idbobjectstore-put-unique-index-constraint-is-atomic in window, worker and sharedworker; the other two changes are known whole-test flips (idbfactory-origin-isolation passes, idbversionchangeevent.any.sharedworker timed out; 5 of 5 alone). New fork test tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/unique-index-keys.any.js: 7 subtests in 4 globals pass with SM, QuickJS and --multiprocess, 20 of 28 fail with the P2 binary; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 134 of 134. A page probe read back from the SQLite file gives the spec's records for multiEntry, placeholders and cursor.update on SM and QuickJS alike. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both: no warnings; `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings` passes. With the P3 review fixes folded in (the keys computed with the transaction inactive): `./mach check` without warnings and nextest 122 passed, 1 skipped at this commit; the new fork test tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/index-keys-page-code.any.js (2 subtests in 4 globals: requests from an Array.prototype getter that put() or cursor.update() reach throw a TransactionInactiveError, and only the outer record is stored) passes at the branch tip on SM, QuickJS and the DAYU200, and the P3.3 binary fails all 8.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; designed and wrote the index key computation, the placeholder rules, the backend index maintenance and the tests, and ran the checks; after the P3 review, computed the keys with the transaction inactive and wrote the page-code fork test)
